{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12235","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12235","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12235","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hangzhan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Hangzhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitao","familyName":"Luan","name":"Luan, Sitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianwei","familyName":"Ni","name":"Ni, Tianwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guillaume","familyName":"Rabusseau","name":"Rabusseau, Guillaume","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Reihaneh","familyName":"Rabbany","name":"Rabbany, Reihaneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Doina","familyName":"Precup","name":"Precup, Doina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Hamdaqa","name":"Hamdaqa, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RL Fine-Tuning Heals OOD Forgetting in SFT"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T21:40:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:00:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-01T13:50:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:33:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-08T20:26:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-12T00:13:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) followed by Reinforcement Learning (RL) is a standard post-training recipe for improving Large Language Models (LLM) reasoning, but why it works remains unclear. We revisit the common claim that ``SFT memorizes, RL generalizes'' through checkpoint-wise analyses of in-distribution (ID) and out-of-distribution (OOD) reasoning. We find that OOD performance often peaks early during SFT and then declines despite continued improvement in ID reasoning. RL typically does not surpass this early SFT peak; rather, it restores OOD capability lost during later SFT, and only from a bounded range of SFT checkpoints. Further spectral analysis shows that this forgetting-and-recovery pattern correlates with rotations of singular vectors, while singular values remain largely stable. These findings suggest a more precise view of post-training dynamics: SFT can forget, RL can recover, and controlling singular-vector rotation may improve OOD robustness. Code is available at \\href{https://github.com/jinhangzhan/RL\\_Heals\\_SFT.git}{https://github.com/jinhangzhan/RL\\_Heals\\_SFT}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"31 pages, 22 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12235","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:35:48Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:35:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-12T05:02:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12464","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12464","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12464","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ryan","familyName":"Lucas","name":"Lucas, Ryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kayhan","familyName":"Behdin","name":"Behdin, Kayhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhipeng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingquan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Qingquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Shao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahul","familyName":"Mazumder","name":"Mazumder, Rahul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Models Can be Accurately Pruned Via Chain-of-Thought Reconstruction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T21:19:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:09:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-02T10:56:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:29:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning language models such as DeepSeek-R1 produce long chain-of-thought traces during inference time which make them costly to deploy at scale. We show that using compression techniques such as neural network pruning produces greater performance loss than in typical language modeling tasks, and in some cases can make the model slower since they cause the model to produce more thinking tokens but with worse performance. We show that this is partly due to the fact that standard LLM pruning methods often focus on input reconstruction, whereas reasoning is a decode-dominated task. We introduce a simple, drop-in fix: during pruning we jointly reconstruct activations from the input and the model's on-policy chain-of-thought traces. This \"Reasoning-Aware Compression\" (RAC) integrates seamlessly into existing pruning workflows such as SparseGPT, and boosts their performance significantly. Code reproducing the results in the paper can be found at: https://github.com/RyanLucas3/RAC"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12464","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:01Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takuma","familyName":"Akimoto","name":"Akimoto, Takuma","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jae-Hyung","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Jae-Hyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ralf","familyName":"Metzler","name":"Metzler, Ralf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomoshige","familyName":"Miyaguchi","name":"Miyaguchi, Tomoshige","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Uneyama","name":"Uneyama, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eiji","familyName":"Yamamoto","name":"Yamamoto, Eiji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Anomalous statistics in the Langevin equation with fluctuating diffusivity: from Brownian yet non-Gaussian diffusion to anomalous diffusion and ergodicity breaking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T01:56:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:15:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:27:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-01T00:15:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1361-6633/ae358c","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusive motion is a fundamental transport mechanism in physical and biological systems, governing dynamics across a wide range of scales -- from molecular transport to animal foraging. In many complex systems, however, diffusion deviates from classical Brownian behaviour, exhibiting striking phenomena such as Brownian yet non-Gaussian diffusion (BYNGD) and anomalous diffusion. BYNGD describes a frequently observed statistical feature characterised by the coexistence of linear mean-square displacement (MSD) and non-Gaussian displacement distributions. Anomalous diffusion, in contrast, involves a nonlinear time dependence of the MSD and often reflects mechanisms such as trapping, viscoelasticity, heterogeneity, or active processes. Both phenomena challenge the conventional framework based on constant diffusivity and Gaussian statistics. This review focuses the theoretical modelling of such behaviour via the Langevin equation with fluctuating diffusivity (LEFD) -- a flexible stochastic framework that captures essential features of diffusion in heterogeneous media. LEFD not only accounts for BYNGD but also naturally encompasses a wide range of anomalous transport phenomena, including subdiffusion, ageing, and weak ergodicity breaking. Ergodicity is discussed in terms of the correspondence between time and ensemble averages, as well as the trajectory-to-trajectory variability of time-averaged observables. The review further highlights the empirical relevance of LEFD and related models in explaining diverse experimental observations and underscores their value to uncovering the physical mechanisms governing transport in complex systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"52 pages, 16 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:29Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-01T02:31:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13021","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13021","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13021","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phung Duc","familyName":"Luong","name":"Luong, Phung Duc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le Tran Gia","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Le Tran Gia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nguyen Vu Khai","familyName":"Tam","name":"Tam, Nguyen Vu Khai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong Huu Nguyen","familyName":"Khoa","name":"Khoa, Dong Huu Nguyen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nguyen Huu","familyName":"Quyen","name":"Quyen, Nguyen Huu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van-Hau","familyName":"Pham","name":"Pham, Van-Hau","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Phan The","familyName":"Duy","name":"Duy, Phan The","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"xOffense: An Autonomous Multi-Agent Framework for Penetration Testing with Domain-Adapted Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:45:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:44:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-27T11:42:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-28T01:36:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work introduces xOffense, an AI-driven, multi-agent penetration testing framework that shifts the process from labor-intensive, expert-driven manual efforts to fully automated, machine-executable workflows capable of scaling seamlessly with computational infrastructure. At its core, xOffense leverages a fine-tuned, mid-scale open-source LLM (Qwen3-32B) to drive reasoning and decision-making in penetration testing. The framework assigns specialized agents to reconnaissance, vulnerability scanning, and exploitation, with an orchestration layer ensuring seamless coordination across phases. Fine-tuning on Chain-of-Thought penetration testing data further enables the model to generate precise tool commands and perform consistent multi-step reasoning. We evaluate xOffense on two rigorous benchmarks: AutoPenBench and AI-Pentest-Benchmark. The results demonstrate that xOffense consistently outperforms contemporary methods, achieving a sub-task completion rate of 79.17%, decisively surpassing leading systems such as VulnBot and PentestGPT. These findings highlight the potential of domain-adapted mid-scale LLMs, when embedded within structured multi-agent orchestration, to deliver superior, cost-efficient, and reproducible solutions for autonomous penetration testing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13021","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:54:00Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:54:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-28T04:04:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12837","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12837","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12837","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kumpei","familyName":"Shiraishi","name":"Shiraishi, Kumpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emi","familyName":"Minamitani","name":"Minamitani, Emi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking thermostat algorithms in molecular dynamics simulations of a binary Lennard-Jones glass-former model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Soft Condensed Matter (cond-mat.soft)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:59:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-24T00:10:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1063/5.0327041","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A systematic comparison was carried out to assess the influence of representative thermostat methods in constant-temperature molecular dynamics simulations. The thermostat schemes considered include the Nosé--Hoover thermostat and its chain generalisation, the Bussi velocity rescaling method, and several implementations of the Langevin dynamics. Using a binary Lennard-Jones liquid as a model glass former, we investigated how the sampling of physical observables, such as particle velocities and potential energy, responds to changes in time step across these thermostats. While the Nosé--Hoover chain and Bussi thermostats provide reliable temperature control, a pronounced time-step dependence was observed in the potential energy. Amongst the Langevin methods, the Grønbech-Jensen--Farago scheme provided the most consistent sampling of both temperature and potential energy. Nonetheless, Langevin dynamics typically incurs approximately twice the computational cost due to the overhead of random number generation, and exhibits a systematic decrease in diffusion coefficients with increasing friction. This study presents a broad comparison of thermostat methods using a binary Lennard-Jones glass-former model, offering practical guidance for the choice of thermostats in classical molecular dynamics simulations. These findings provide useful insights for diverse applications, including glass transition, phase separation, and nucleation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"25 pages, 14 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12837","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:43Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-24T02:13:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13281","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13281","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13281","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent","familyName":"Siu","name":"Siu, Vincent","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan W.","familyName":"Henry","name":"Henry, Nathan W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas","familyName":"Crispino","name":"Crispino, Nicholas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawn","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Dawn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenguang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RepIt: Steering Language Models with Concept-Specific Refusal Vectors"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:58:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T07:29:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:29:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T05:13:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:27:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-20T21:21:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-22T00:12:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-21T07:53:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:40:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current safety evaluations of language models rely on benchmark-based assessments that may miss localized vulnerabilities. We present RepIt, a simple and data-efficient framework for isolating concept-specific representations in LM activations. While existing steering methods already achieve high attack success rates through broad interventions, RepIt enables a more concerning capability: selective suppression of refusal on targeted concepts while preserving refusal elsewhere. Across five frontier LMs, RepIt produces evaluation-evading model organisms with semantic backdoors, answering questions related to weapons of mass destruction while still scoring as safe on standard benchmarks. We find the edit of the steering vector localizes to just 100-200 residual dimensions, and robust concept vectors can be extracted from as few as a dozen examples on a single RTX A6000, highlighting how targeted, hard-to-detect modifications can exploit evaluation blind spots with minimal resources. Through demonstrating precise concept disentanglement, this work exposes vulnerabilities in current safety evaluation practices and demonstrates a need for more comprehensive, representation aware assessments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13281","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:08Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:29:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13223","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13223","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13223","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Veronika","familyName":"Chronholm","name":"Chronholm, Veronika","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tristan","familyName":"Pryer","name":"Pryer, Tristan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometry, Energy and Sensitivity in Stochastic Proton Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T16:31:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:56:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-20T19:33:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-22T00:08:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We develop numerical schemes and sensitivity methods for stochastic models of proton transport that couple energy loss, range straggling and angular diffusion. For the energy equation we introduce a logarithmic Milstein scheme that guarantees positivity and achieves strong order one convergence. For the angular dynamics we construct a Lie-group integrator. The combined method maintains the natural geometric invariants of the system.\n We formulate dose deposition as a regularised path-dependent functional, obtaining a pathwise sensitivity estimator that is consistent and implementable. Numerical experiments confirm that the proposed schemes achieve the expected convergence rates and provide stable estimates of dose sensitivities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"29 pages, 12 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13223","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:58:45Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:58:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-22T02:29:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13047","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13047","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13047","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nolan","familyName":"Platt","name":"Platt, Nolan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pragyansmita","familyName":"Nayak","name":"Nayak, Pragyansmita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Model Synthetic Training for Mission-Critical Small Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T50 68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:04:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:48:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-12T16:39:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:09:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/fllm67465.2025.11391217","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across many domains, yet their application to specialized fields remains constrained by the scarcity and complexity of domain-specific training data. We present a novel approach that achieves a 261x cost reduction for maritime intelligence by using LLMs as one-time teachers rather than using them directly for inference. Our method transforms 3.2 billion Automatic Identification System (AIS) vessel tracking records into 21,543 synthetic question and answer pairs through multi-model generation (GPT-4o and o3-mini), preventing overfitting and ensuring accurate reasoning. The resulting fine-tuned Qwen2.5-7B model achieves 75% accuracy on maritime tasks, while being substantially cheaper than using a larger model for inference. We show that smaller, cheaper models -- when fine tuned properly -- can provide similar accuracy compared to larger models that are prohibitively expensive. Our work contributes to the growing field of synthetic dataset generation for specialized AI applications and presents a highly reproducible framework for domains where manual annotation is infeasible. Beyond expanding research in the growing field of specialized small language models, our approach has immediate applications in maritime safety, security operations, and vessel traffic management systems in various industries."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages. Accepted as a full paper to the 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (IEEE FLLM) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13047","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:54:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:54:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:17:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12694","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12694","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12694","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiadong","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Jiadong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Wenjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaoyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhaoyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Soft Graph Transformer for MIMO Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T05:42:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T14:55:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:14:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T06:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:27:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-23T07:57:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:23:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-13T09:03:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-14T01:37:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose the Soft Graph Transformer (SGT), a soft-input-soft-output neural architecture designed for MIMO detection. While Maximum Likelihood (ML) detection achieves optimal accuracy, its exponential complexity makes it infeasible in large systems, and conventional message-passing algorithms rely on asymptotic assumptions that often fail in finite dimensions. Recent Transformer-based detectors show strong performance but typically overlook the MIMO factor graph structure and cannot exploit prior soft information. SGT addresses these limitations by combining self-attention, which encodes contextual dependencies within symbol and constraint subgraphs, with graph-aware cross-attention, which performs structured message passing across subgraphs. Its soft-input interface allows the integration of auxiliary priors, producing effective soft outputs while maintaining computational efficiency. Experiments demonstrate that SGT achieves near-ML performance and offers a flexible and interpretable framework for receiver systems that leverage soft priors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages with 3 figures and 2 tables, Accepted by IEEE ICASSP 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12694","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:20Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-14T04:17:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglei","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Honglei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengqian","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shengqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixuan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kaixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanheng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yuanheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongbin","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Dongbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Empowering Multi-Robot Cooperation via Sequential World Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:52:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T02:55:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:25:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-04T14:43:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T00:33:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Model-based reinforcement learning (MBRL) has achieved remarkable success in robotics due to its high sample efficiency and planning capability. However, extending MBRL to physical multi-robot cooperation remains challenging due to the complexity of joint dynamics. To address this challenge, we propose the Sequential World Model (SeqWM), a novel framework that integrates the sequential paradigm into multi-robot MBRL. SeqWM employs independent, autoregressive agent-wise world models to represent joint dynamics, where each agent generates its future trajectory and plans its actions based on the predictions of its predecessors. This design lowers modeling complexity and enables the emergence of advanced cooperative behaviors through explicit intention sharing. Experiments on Bi-DexHands and Multi-Quadruped demonstrate that SeqWM outperforms existing state-of-the-art model-based and model-free baselines in both overall performance and sample efficiency, while exhibiting advanced cooperative behaviors such as predictive adaptation, temporal alignment, and role division. Furthermore, SeqWM has been successfully deployed on physical quadruped robots, validating its effectiveness in real-world multi-robot systems. Demos and code are available at: https://github.com/zhaozijie2022/seqwm"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:55:48Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:55:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-07T03:25:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12643","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12643","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12643","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beidan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Beidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengqiu","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhengqiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanjun","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Quanjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learn to Relax with Large Language Models: Solving Constraint Optimization Problems via Bidirectional Coevolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T03:59:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:21:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T06:58:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:26:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T06:46:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-03T00:29:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-03T11:27:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:38:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based optimization has recently shown promise for autonomous problem solving, yet most approaches still cast LLMs as passive constraint checkers rather than proactive strategy designers, limiting their effectiveness on complex Constraint Optimization Problems (COPs). To address this, we present AutoCO, an end-to-end Automated Constraint Optimization method that tightly couples operations-research principles of constraint relaxation with LLM reasoning. A core innovation is a unified triple-representation that binds relaxation strategies, algorithmic principles, and executable codes. This design enables the LLM to synthesize, justify, and instantiate relaxation strategies that are both principled and executable. To navigate fragmented solution spaces, AutoCO employs a bidirectional global-local coevolution mechanism, synergistically coupling Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS) for global relaxation-trajectory exploration with Evolutionary Algorithms (EAs) for local solution intensification. This continuous exchange of priors and feedback explicitly balances diversification and intensification, thus preventing premature convergence. Extensive experiments on three challenging COP benchmarks validate AutoCO's consistent effectiveness and superior performance, especially in hard regimes where current methods degrade. Results highlight AutoCO as a principled and effective path toward proactive, verifiable LLM-driven optimization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12643","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:10Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-06T01:48:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12822","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12822","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12822","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Curt","familyName":"Stutsman","name":"Stutsman, Curt","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eliot W.","familyName":"Robson","name":"Robson, Eliot W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhishek K.","familyName":"Umrawal","name":"Umrawal, Abhishek K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Pressure-Based Diffusion Model for Influence Maximization on Social Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"G.2.1; G.2.2; G.3; H.3.4; H.3.5; I.2.0; J.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"05C85, 60J60, 68R05, 68R10, 68T01, 90C35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:47:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:33:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-16T12:05:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-19T01:33:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T01:21:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-03T00:15:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In many real-world scenarios, an individual's local social network carries significant influence over the opinions they form and subsequently propagate. In this paper, we propose a novel diffusion model -- the Pressure Threshold model (PT) -- for dynamically simulating the spread of influence through a social network. This model extends the popular Linear Threshold (LT) model by adjusting a node's outgoing influence in proportion to the influence it receives from its activated neighbors. We examine the Influence Maximization (IM) problem under this framework, which involves selecting seed nodes that yield maximal graph coverage after a diffusion process, and describe how the problem manifests under the PT model. Experiments on real-world networks, supported by enhancements to the open-source network-diffusion library CyNetDiff, reveal that greedy IM under PT can yield seed sets distinct from those under LT. Furthermore, the analyses show that densely connected networks amplify pressure effects far more strongly than sparse networks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 8 figures, and 2 tables; Accepted for presentation at the 20th International AAAI Conference on Web and Social Media (ICWSM 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12822","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:22Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-03T02:14:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13007","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13007","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13007","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qitan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Qitan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuantao","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yuantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ReTrack: Data Unlearning in Diffusion Models through Redirecting the Denoising Trajectory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T12:20:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:43:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-29T15:08:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:03:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models excel at generating high-quality, diverse images but suffer from training data memorization, raising critical privacy and safety concerns. Data unlearning has emerged to mitigate this issue by removing the influence of specific data without retraining from scratch. We propose ReTrack, a fast and effective data unlearning method for diffusion models. ReTrack employs importance sampling to construct a more efficient fine-tuning loss, which we approximate by retaining only dominant terms. This yields an interpretable objective that redirects denoising trajectories toward the $k$-nearest neighbors, enabling efficient unlearning while preserving generative quality. Experiments on MNIST T-Shirt, CelebA-HQ, CIFAR-10, and Stable Diffusion show that ReTrack achieves state-of-the-art performance, striking the best trade-off between unlearning strength and generation quality preservation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages, 12 figures, accepted by AISTATS 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13007","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:53:40Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:53:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:01:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12554","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12554","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12554","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haichao","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Haichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao-Yu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiao-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multimodal Graph Network Modeling for Human-Object Interaction Detection with PDE Graph Diffusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T01:17:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:13:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-14T13:10:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-17T01:42:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-30T08:23:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-31T01:29:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing GNN-based Human-Object Interaction (HOI) detection methods rely on simple MLPs to fuse instance features and propagate information. However, this mechanism is largely empirical and lack of targeted information propagation process. To address this problem, we propose Multimodal Graph Network Modeling (MGNM) for HOI detection with Partial Differential Equation (PDE) graph diffusion. Specifically, we first design a multimodal graph network framework that explicitly models the HOI detection task within a four-stage graph structure. Next, we propose a novel PDE diffusion mechanism to facilitate information propagation within this graph. This mechanism leverages multimodal features to propaganda information via a white-box PDE diffusion equation. Furthermore, we design a variational information squeezing (VIS) mechanism to further refine the multimodal features extracted from CLIP, thereby mitigating the impact of noise inherent in pretrained Vision-Language Models. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our MGNM achieves state-of-the-art performance on two widely used benchmarks: HICO-DET and V-COCO. Moreover, when integrated with a more advanced object detector, our method yields significant performance gains while maintaining an effective balance between rare and non-rare categories."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12554","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:06Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-31T04:01:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13313","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13313","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13313","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xixi","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xixi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yida","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yida","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liwen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Litu","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Litu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huifeng","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Huifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongwang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhongwang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinmiao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Xinmiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingchu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Dingchu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengjun","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Pengjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minhao","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Minhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingren","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jingren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ReSum: Unlocking Long-Horizon Search Intelligence via Context Summarization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:57:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:59:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T15:51:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T01:00:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T06:50:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-27T00:32:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based web agents excel at knowledge-intensive tasks but face a fundamental conflict between the need for extensive exploration and the constraints of limited context windows. Current solutions typically rely on architectural modifications, e.g., internal memory tokens, which break compatibility with pre-existing agents and necessitate costly end-to-end retraining. To overcome these limitations, we introduce ReSum, a lightweight, plug-and-play paradigm that enables unbounded exploration by periodically invoking an external tool to condense interaction histories into compact summaries. Although this paradigm functions without training, standard agents are not inherently aligned to reason over such compressed contexts. To bridge this gap, we propose ReSum-GRPO, which adapts Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) via advantage broadcasting to propagate final rewards across segmented trajectories, enabling credit assignments over long-horizons. Extensive experiments show that ReSum achieves a 4.5% improvement over ReAct in training-free settings, with ReSum-GRPO yielding a further 8.2% gain. Notably, with only 1K training samples, a ReSum-enhanced 30B agent achieves competitive performance with leading open-source models, showing ReSum's effectiveness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13313","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T02:00:55Z","registered":"2025-09-17T02:00:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-27T02:21:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13093","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13093","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13093","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujie","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Yujie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiwan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shiwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GLAD: Global-Local Aware Dynamic Mixture-of-Experts for Multi-Talker ASR"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T13:51:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:48:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-18T06:53:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-19T00:24:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-22T14:58:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:40:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-19T01:00:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-20T00:26:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"End-to-end multi-talker automatic speech recognition (MTASR) faces significant challenges in accurately transcribing overlapping speech. A critical bottleneck is that speaker-specific acoustic characteristics, which are essential for distinguishing overlapping speech, are often diluted in deep network layers. To address this, we propose the Global-Local Aware Dynamic Mixture-of-Experts (GLAD) architecture. GLAD introduces a novel routing mechanism that dynamically fuses speaker-aware global context with fine-grained local acoustic details to adaptively guide expert selection. Experiments on the LibriSpeechMix and CH109 datasets demonstrate that GLAD significantly outperforms existing Serialized Output Training (SOT)-based MTASR approaches, exhibiting exceptional robustness in challenging, high-overlap scenarios. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work to apply a global-local fusion MoE strategy to MTASR."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been submitted to Interspeech 2026 for review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13093","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:55:45Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:55:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-20T02:37:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12702","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12702","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12702","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongrui","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Hongrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xunlan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xunlan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boris","familyName":"Ivanovic","name":"Ivanovic, Boris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Negar","familyName":"Mehr","name":"Mehr, Negar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"UDON: Uncertainty-weighted Distributed Optimization for Multi-Robot Neural Implicit Mapping under Extreme Communication Constraints"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T05:52:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:25:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-19T16:54:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-20T01:15:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-robot mapping with neural implicit representations enables the compact reconstruction of complex environments. However, it demands robustness against communication challenges like packet loss and limited bandwidth. While prior works have introduced various mechanisms to mitigate communication disruptions, performance degradation still occurs under extremely low communication success rates. This paper presents UDON, a real-time multi-agent neural implicit mapping framework that introduces a novel uncertainty-weighted distributed optimization to achieve high-quality mapping under severe communication deterioration. The uncertainty weighting prioritizes more reliable portions of the map, while the distributed optimization isolates and penalizes mapping disagreement between individual pairs of communicating agents. We conduct extensive experiments on standard benchmark datasets and real-world robot hardware. We demonstrate that UDON significantly outperforms existing baselines, maintaining high-fidelity reconstructions and consistent scene representations even under extreme communication degradation (as low as 1% success rate)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IEEE International Conference on Robotics and Automation (ICRA 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12702","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:46:31Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:46:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-20T02:37:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12431","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12431","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12431","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinzhanghao","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yinzhanghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tsung-Han","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Tsung-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicola","familyName":"Lanatà","name":"Lanatà, Nicola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Neural-Quantum-States Impurity Solver for Quantum Embedding Problems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T20:33:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:07:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-13T17:19:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-16T01:01:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural quantum states (NQS) have emerged as a promising approach to solve second-quantized Hamiltonians, because of their scalability and flexibility. In this work, we design and benchmark an NQS impurity solver for the quantum embedding (QE) methods, focusing on the ghost Gutzwiller Approximation (gGA) framework. We introduce a graph transformer-based NQS framework able to represent arbitrarily connected impurity orbitals of the embedding Hamiltonian (EH) and develop an error control mechanism to stabilize iterative updates throughout the QE loops. We validate the accuracy of our approach with benchmark gGA calculations of the Anderson Lattice Model, yielding results in excellent agreement with the exact diagonalisation impurity solver. Finally, our analysis of the computational budget reveals the method's principal bottleneck to be the high-accuracy sampling of physical observables required by the embedding loop, rather than the NQS variational optimization, directly highlighting the critical need for more efficient inference techniques."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages main text, and 4 figures. Note that YinZhangHao Zhou and Zhanghao Zhouyin are the same person, I use them both"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12431","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:40:16Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:40:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-16T02:07:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12583","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12583","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12583","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bang","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Bang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peijun","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Peijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarong","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiarong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Audio-Visual Target Speaker Extraction with Emotion-Aware Multiple Enrollment Fusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:21:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:16:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-24T09:08:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-25T00:36:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T03:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:23:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Audio-Visual Target Speaker Extraction (AVTSE) is crucial for cocktail party scenarios. Leveraging multiple cues --such as utterance-level speaker embeddings or steady face images, and frame-level lip motion or facial expression features --can significantly improve performance. However, real-world applications often suffer from intermittent signal loss, especially for frame-level cues. This paper systematically investigates the robustness of multi-enrollment fusion under varying degrees of modality missing. Results show that while full multimodal fusion excels under ideal conditions, its performance degrades sharply when encountering unseen modalities missing during the testing. Crucially, training with a high missing rate dramatically enhances robustness, maintaining stable performance even under severe test-time modality missing. We demonstrate that fusing the complementary one frame of face image with frame-level lip features achieves both strong performance and robustness for the AVTSE task. The model and codes are shared."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"submitted to Interspeech 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12583","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:46Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:24:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13251","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13251","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13251","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiwen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kaiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhua","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Wenhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixiong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Weixiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Model Assisted Automated Algorithm Generation and Evolution via Meta-black-box optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T17:02:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:57:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-19T01:51:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-11T00:55:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.eswa.2026.131756","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Meta-black-box optimization has been significantly advanced through the use of large language models (LLMs), yet in fancy on constrained evolutionary optimization. In this work, AwesomeDE is proposed that leverages LLMs as the strategy of meta-optimizer to generate update rules for constrained evolutionary algorithm without human intervention. On the meanwhile, $RTO^2H$ framework is introduced for standardize prompt design of LLMs. The meta-optimizer is trained on a diverse set of constrained optimization problems. Key components, including prompt design and iterative refinement, are systematically analyzed to determine their impact on design quality. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed approach outperforms existing methods in terms of computational efficiency and solution accuracy. Furthermore, AwesomeDE is shown to generalize well across distinct problem domains, suggesting its potential for broad applicability. This research contributes to the field by providing a scalable and data-driven methodology for automated constrained algorithm design, while also highlighting limitations and directions for future work."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13251","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:59:25Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:59:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-11T02:33:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.13172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.13172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruifei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Ruifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Long","name":"Long, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijuan","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Huijuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yelu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yelu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bisheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Bisheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WHU-STree: A Multi-modal Benchmark Dataset for Street Tree Inventory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T15:23:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:47:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.isprsjprs.2026.02.011","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Street trees are vital to urban livability, providing ecological and social benefits. Establishing a detailed, accurate, and dynamically updated street tree inventory has become essential for optimizing these multifunctional assets within space-constrained urban environments. Given that traditional field surveys are time-consuming and labor-intensive, automated surveys utilizing Mobile Mapping Systems (MMS) offer a more efficient solution. However, existing MMS-acquired tree datasets are limited by small-scale scene, limited annotation, or single modality, restricting their utility for comprehensive analysis. To address these limitations, we introduce WHU-STree, a cross-city, richly annotated, and multi-modal urban street tree dataset. Collected across two distinct cities, WHU-STree integrates synchronized point clouds and high-resolution images, encompassing 21,007 annotated tree instances across 50 species and 2 morphological parameters. Leveraging the unique characteristics, WHU-STree concurrently supports over 10 tasks related to street tree inventory. We benchmark representative baselines for two key tasks--tree species classification and individual tree segmentation. Extensive experiments and in-depth analysis demonstrate the significant potential of multi-modal data fusion and underscore cross-domain applicability as a critical prerequisite for practical algorithm deployment. In particular, we identify key challenges and outline potential future works for fully exploiting WHU-STree, encompassing multi-modal fusion, multi-task collaboration, cross-domain generalization, spatial pattern learning, and Multi-modal Large Language Model for street tree asset management. The WHU-STree dataset is accessible at: https://github.com/WHU-USI3DV/WHU-STree."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.13172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-17T01:57:35Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:57:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:49:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12817","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12817","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12817","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SAGA: Selective Adaptive Gating for Efficient and Expressive Linear Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:36:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:33:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T03:15:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:25:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Transformer architecture excel at modeling long-range dependencies contributing to its widespread adoption in vision tasks the quadratic complexity of softmax-based attention mechanisms imposes a major bottleneck, particularly when processing high-resolution images. Linear attention presents a promising alternative by reformulating the attention computation from $(QK)V$ to $Q(KV)$, thereby reducing the complexity from $\\mathcal{O}(N^2)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ while preserving the global receptive field. However, most existing methods compress historical key-value (KV) information uniformly, which can lead to feature redundancy and the loss of directional alignment with the query (Q). This uniform compression results in low-rank $KV$ feature maps, contributing to a performance gap compared to softmax attention. To mitigate this limitation, we propose \\textbf{S}elective \\textbf{A}daptive \\textbf{GA}ting for Efficient and Expressive Linear Attention (SAGA) , which introduces input-adaptive learnable gates to selectively modulate information aggregation into the $KV$ feature map. These gates enhance semantic diversity and alleviate the low-rank constraint inherent in conventional linear attention. Additionally, we propose an efficient Hadamard-product decomposition method for gate computation, which introduces no additional memory overhead. Experiments demonstrate that SAGA achieves a 1.76$\\times$ improvement in throughput and a 2.69$\\times$ reduction in peak GPU memory compared to PVT-T at a resolution of $1280 \\times 1280$. Moreover, it improves top-1 accuracy by up to 4.4\\% on the ImageNet dataset, demonstrating both computational efficiency and model effectiveness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12817","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:16Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:49:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12890","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12890","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12890","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Malte","familyName":"Probst","name":"Probst, Malte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raphael","familyName":"Wenzel","name":"Wenzel, Raphael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monica","familyName":"Dasi","name":"Dasi, Monica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Responsibility and Engagement -- Evaluating Interactions in Social Robot Navigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T09:44:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T14:25:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-05T11:57:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-06T01:51:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In Social Robot Navigation (SRN), the availability of meaningful metrics is crucial for evaluating trajectories from human-robot interactions. In the SRN context, such interactions often relate to resolving conflicts between two or more agents. Correspondingly, the shares to which agents contribute to the resolution of such conflicts are important. This paper builds on recent work, which proposed a Responsibility metric capturing such shares. We extend this framework in two directions: First, we model the conflict buildup phase by introducing a time normalization. Second, we propose the related Engagement metric, which captures how the agents' actions intensify a conflict. In a comprehensive series of simulated scenarios with dyadic, group and crowd interactions, we show that the metrics carry meaningful information about the cooperative resolution of conflicts in interactions. They can be used to assess behavior quality and foresightedness. We extensively discuss applicability, design choices and limitations of the proposed metrics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the 2026 IEEE International Conference on Robotics \u0026amp; Automation (ICRA)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12890","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:50:58Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:50:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-06T03:25:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12813","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12813","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12813","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyue","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Junyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaozhen","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Xiaozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Perception and Planning: Towards End-to-End Planning for Signal Temporal Logic Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T08:31:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:32:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T04:10:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:59:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the task and motion planning problem for Signal Temporal Logic (STL) specifications in robotics. Existing STL methods rely on pre-defined maps or mobility representations, which are ineffective in unstructured real-world environments. We propose the \\emph{Structured-MoE STL Planner} (\\textbf{S-MSP}), a differentiable framework that maps synchronized multi-view camera observations and an STL specification directly to a feasible trajectory. S-MSP integrates STL constraints within a unified pipeline, trained with a composite loss that combines trajectory reconstruction and STL robustness. A \\emph{structure-aware} Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) model enables horizon-aware specialization by projecting sub-tasks into temporally anchored embeddings. We evaluate S-MSP using a high-fidelity simulation of factory-logistics scenarios with temporally constrained tasks. Experiments show that S-MSP outperforms single-expert baselines in STL satisfaction and trajectory feasibility. A rule-based \\emph{safety filter} at inference improves physical executability without compromising logical correctness, showcasing the practicality of the approach."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12813","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:49:10Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:49:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:42:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sasi Kiran","familyName":"Gaddipati","name":"Gaddipati, Sasi Kiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhana","familyName":"Keya","name":"Keya, Farhana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gollam","familyName":"Rabby","name":"Rabby, Gollam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sören","familyName":"Auer","name":"Auer, Sören","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AISSISTANT: Human-AI Collaborative Review and Perspective Research Workflows in Data Science"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T15:50:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-01T09:54:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:06:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"High-quality scientific review and perspective papers require substantial time and effort, limiting researchers' ability to synthesize emerging knowledge. While Large Language Models (LLMs) leverage AI Scientists for scientific workflows, existing frameworks focus primarily on autonomous workflows with very limited human intervention. We introduce AIssistant, the first open-source agentic framework for Human--AI collaborative generation of scientific perspectives and review research in data science. AIssistant employs specialized LLM-driven agents augmented with external scholarly tools and allows human intervention throughout the workflow. The framework consists of two main multi-agent systems: Research Workflow with seven agents and a Paper Writing Workflow with eight agents. We conducted a comprehensive evaluation with both human expert reviewers and LLM-based assessment following NeurIPS standards. Our experiments show that OpenAI o1 achieves the highest quality scores on chain-of-thought prompting with augmented Literature Search tools. We also conducted a Human--AI interaction survey with results showing a 65.7\\% time savings. We believe that our work establishes a baseline for Human--AI collaborative scientific workflow for review and perspective research in data science, demonstrating that agent-augmented pipelines substantially reduce effort while maintaining research integrity through strategic human oversight."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:52Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:42:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":281,"totalPages":12,"page":2},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=3\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-16T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-17T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+agent+OR+agents+OR+diffusion+OR+multimodal+OR+GPU+OR+MoE+OR+%22state+space%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+%22vision+language%22%29"}}